.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_PGVECTOR 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_pgvector
.Nd pgvector support in pg_laswell: readings and refusals for vector indexes
.Sh DESCRIPTION
pgvector support is a
.Em build-time module ,
like Citus support
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_citus 7 .
Unlike Citus it adds no intent kinds.
Everything pgvector provides is reached through core kinds:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Cm create_extension
with
.Va name
.Ql vector ;
.It
a
.Li vector(n) ,
.Li halfvec(n) ,
.Li sparsevec(n)
or
.Li bit(n)
column, as the
.Va type
of
.Cm create_table ,
.Cm add_column
or
.Cm alter_column_type ;
.It
an HNSW or IVFFlat index, as
.Cm create_index
with
.Va method
.Ql hnsw
or
.Ql ivfflat ,
an operator class per column, and storage parameters in
.Va with .
.El
.Pp
What the module adds is what core cannot know: which of those indexes pgvector
will refuse.
Each one is an error PostgreSQL would raise anyway, but an HNSW index on a large
table is built with
.Ic CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ,
which the dry run cannot rehearse, and a concurrent build refused at execution
has already scanned the table once and left an invalid index behind.
The module says it before anything runs.
.Pp
A binary that carries the module says so:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pg_laswell_mcp --version
0.1.3
modules: citus,pg_cron,pgvector,timescaledb
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy Versions.
Measured on PostgreSQL 18.6 with pgvector 0.8.6.
The operator classes and the access methods' capabilities are read from the
server, so a pgvector that adds a class is not refused for using it.
The dimension limits and the storage parameter bounds are not in any catalog;
they are the measured values, and each refusal quotes the pgvector error it
pre-empts.
.Sh AN HNSW INDEX
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "kind": "create_index", "schema": "public", "table": "documents",
  "name": "documents_embedding_idx", "method": "hnsw",
  "columns": [ { "name": "embedding", "opclass": "vector_cosine_ops" } ],
  "with": { "m": 16, "ef_construction": 64 },
  "comment": "Nearest documents by cosine distance." }
.Ed
.Pp
The operator class is the distance the index serves, and it must match the
operator queries order by:
.Li <->
.Pq l2 ,
.Li <#>
.Pq inner product ,
.Li <=>
.Pq cosine ,
.Li <+>
.Pq l1 ,
and for
.Li bit
columns
.Li <~>
.Pq Hamming
and
.Li <%>
.Pq Jaccard .
An index built for one distance is not used by a query ordering by another.
.Pp
Whether the build is plain or concurrent is core's decision, by the same rule
as any index
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Cm create_index .
.Sh READINGS
Taken only where the extension is installed in the target database; absent
otherwise.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va version
.Li pg_extension.extversion .
.It Va opclasses
Every operator class of
.Li hnsw
and
.Li ivfflat :
the type it serves and whether it is the default.
On 0.8.6, hnsw has fourteen and no default; ivfflat has seven, and
.Li vector_l2_ops
is its default.
.It Va methods
.Fn pg_indexam_has_property
for
.Li can_unique
and
.Li can_multi_col .
Both are false for both methods.
.It Va dynamic_shared_memory_type
Where a parallel build's shared memory comes from;
.Ql posix
is
.Pa /dev/shm .
.El
.Pp
The advisories also read core's own readings: the table's row count, the larger
of
.Va reltuples
and
.Va n_live_tup ,
because the first is 0 until an
.Ic ANALYZE ;
.Va max_parallel_maintenance_workers ;
and the
.Va maintenance_work_mem
the step will run with, which core decides
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Sx CAPACITY .
.Pp
When the specification itself creates the extension, there is no reading yet,
and the classes pgvector 0.8.6 declares are used instead.
.Sh REFUSALS
For every
.Cm create_index
with
.Va method
.Ql hnsw
or
.Ql ivfflat .
A column's type is the catalog's, or what an earlier intent of the same
specification declares it to be.
An expression key is checked when it is an explicit cast, as the type it casts
to:
.Li embedding::halfvec(3072) ,
.Li CAST(embedding AS halfvec(3072))
and
.Li binary_quantize(embedding)::bit(1536) ,
the shapes pgvector documents for indexing past the
.Li vector
limit.
Any other expression is left to the server, whose type it is to know.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Too many dimensions
.Qq column cannot have more than N dimensions for hnsw index .
2000 for
.Li vector ,
4000 for
.Li halfvec ,
64000 for
.Li bit ,
for both methods.
.Li sparsevec
has no dimension limit on an HNSW index; its limit is 1000 non-zero elements
per row, a property of the data checked when a row is written.
A
.Li vector
wider than 2000 is usually indexed as
.Li halfvec :
an expression key
.Li (embedding::halfvec(3072))
with a
.Li halfvec_*
class, queried with the same cast.
.It No dimensions
.Qq column does not have dimensions ,
for a column declared as plain
.Li vector .
.It No operator class
hnsw has no default class for any type, so every hnsw column names one.
.It An operator class for another type
.Qq operator class \&"halfvec_l2_ops\&" does not accept data type vector .
.It A type the method cannot index
ivfflat has no
.Li sparsevec
classes; neither method indexes anything but pgvector's types.
.It UNIQUE, or more than one key column
.Qq access method \&"hnsw\&" does not support unique indexes ,
and the same for multicolumn indexes.
A where clause still works, for filtering.
.It Storage parameters
hnsw takes
.Va m
(2 to 100, default 16) and
.Va ef_construction
(4 to 1000, default 64), and
.Va ef_construction
must be at least twice
.Va m
\(em including the default, so
.Li {"m": 40}
alone is refused.
ivfflat takes
.Va lists
(1 to 32768).
Anything else is
.Qq unrecognized parameter .
.El
.Sh ADVISORIES
Not refusals, because none is an error, and not warnings: each rests on a
reading that moves on its own, such as a row count autovacuum rewrites, so it is
an
.Em advisory ,
shown with the plan as
.Ql note:
and left out of
.Va planDigest
.Pq Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 , Sx planMigration .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It An IVFFlat index on an empty or small table
IVFFlat chooses its list centres from the rows present when it is built, and
recalls poorly once the table fills.
Said when the table is empty or created by the same specification, and when it
has fewer rows than lists, which is where pgvector itself says
.Qq ivfflat index created with little data .
Build it in a later specification, after the data is loaded.
.It An HNSW graph larger than maintenance_work_mem
The build then continues on disk, much more slowly: measured, 50 000 rows of
.Li vector(128)
took 41.6 s with 1 MB and 12.1 s with 256 MB.
The size is estimated from rows, dimensions and
.Va m ,
by a rule measured on 0.8.6 at the row where pgvector reports
.Qq hnsw graph no longer fits into maintenance_work_mem :
the vector's own bytes
.Pq 8 + 4 a dimension for Li vector , 2 for Li halfvec , 1/8 for Li bit
plus about 210 + 32 x
.Va m .
Four shapes, from
.Li vector(128)
to
.Li vector(768)
and
.Va m
16 to 32, agree within 2%.
.Pp
The estimate is also what the build is given.
pgvector tells core how much memory the graph needs, and core raises the step's
.Va maintenance_work_mem
toward it \(em in the executor and in the dry run alike \(em within a limit:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
With
.Va maintenance_work_mem_mb
configured, its per-step share is used and never exceeded: the operator decided,
and giving one build more would spend memory the other jobs were promised.
.It
Without it, the limit is
.Em deduced from
.Va shared_buffers :
PostgreSQL's documentation suggests about 25% of a dedicated server's memory for
it, so it is the one figure on every server that says how big the server is.
All concurrent builds together are held to one more
.Va shared_buffers ,
so each gets
.Va shared_buffers
divided by
.Va max_concurrent_jobs ,
and never less than the server's own setting.
An untuned server (128MB) gets 64MB a job \(em no raise \(em which is the safe
side of the guess.
.El
.Pp
The step's reason names what it was raised to and why, and
.Va memory_wanted_by
names pgvector.
The advisory remains when the limit leaves the build short, naming what it
still needs and the setting that would give it all of it.
Not given for
.Li sparsevec ,
whose size is its count of non-zero elements, a property of the data.
.It A parallel HNSW build and /dev/shm
A parallel build allocates the whole
.Va maintenance_work_mem
in dynamic shared memory.
Measured in a container with the default 64 MB
.Pa /dev/shm :
a build with 256 MB failed,
.Qq could not resize shared memory segment ... No space left on device ,
and left an invalid index for the next run to drop.
The size of
.Pa /dev/shm
is not visible from SQL, so this is said whenever it could matter: parallel
maintenance workers allowed,
.Va dynamic_shared_memory_type
.Ql posix ,
and more than 64 MB of
.Va maintenance_work_mem
\(em counted after the raise above, which is the figure the build really
allocates, so a raise that makes this possible is never silent.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pg_laswell 1 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_citus 7 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_pg_cron 7 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_timescaledb 7
